    "reading": {
        "body": "Foundation models are catalogued across nineteen categories, and the largest of them are counted in the thousands, not the dozens. The catalogue spans text, reasoning, multimodal, vision, image, video, audio, music, embedding, OCR, coding, robotics, scientific, medical, legal, and financial models, plus a metadata standard covering release, licence, architecture, context, pricing, performance, and safety.\n\nThe counts cluster at round numbers. Large language, multimodal, image generation, video, audio, music, embedding, OCR, coding, on-device, robotics, medical, legal, and financial models all sit at 1,000, while vision sits at 1,500. Only three categories break the pattern: reasoning at 334, scientific at 808, and small language models at 50. That distribution suggests most categories are capped or rounded rather than counted exactly, so the numbers describe breadth of coverage, not the true size of each field.\n\nThe small language model count is the most informative figure here. Its membership rule is a declared parameter count of four billion or fewer taken from safetensors metadata, and models that do not publish a count cannot appear. Fifty models meet that bar. The on-device category, defined instead by the GGUF packaging format that local runtimes load, reaches 1,000. The gap between the two says that packaging for local execution is far more common than publishing a small parameter count, and that the two definitions of small are not the same set.\n\nWhat this data does not contain is any measure of compute, power, or physical footprint. There are no parameter totals, no training run sizes, no accelerator counts, no data centre locations, and no energy figures. It is a taxonomy of model types with membership rules and counts, and it cannot tell a reader how much compute any of these models required or where that compute ran.",
        "model": "ollama:deepseek-v4.1-flash",
        "generated_on": "2026-10-10"
    },
